<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>Where the appraisal clause comes from in Connecticut</h2>
<p>Connecticut is one of the states whose standard fire policy is written into statute. The appraisal provision in that standard form, and in the homeowners and commercial forms built on top of it, follows a familiar pattern: if you and the company fail to agree on the amount of loss, either side may demand appraisal in writing, each side picks a competent and disinterested appraiser, the two appraisers choose an umpire, and a written agreement signed by any two of the three sets the amount of loss.</p>
<p>That structure matters. A two-signature award is not an opening offer. It fixes the dollar value of the covered loss, which is why the person you name to the panel should be chosen for their estimating discipline, not for how loudly they argue.</p>
<h2>What appraisal clause representation includes</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Policy and demand review.</strong> We read your declarations, the appraisal condition and any letters already exchanged, and confirm the deadline for naming an appraiser before anything else.</li>
<li><strong>A fresh scope.</strong> We walk the property, measure every roof plane and room, and photograph conditions ourselves. We do not start from the carrier’s estimate and edit it.</li>
<li><strong>A line-item valuation.</strong> Our estimate is written in Xactimate using pricing for your Connecticut ZIP code, with code items, matching and overhead and profit handled the way your policy actually reads.</li>
<li><strong>Working sessions with the other appraiser.</strong> Most disagreements shrink sharply once both appraisers compare line items in the same room or on the same roof.</li>
<li><strong>Umpire selection and presentation.</strong> If items stay open, we help pick a qualified umpire and present the remaining differences with photos, measurements and pricing support.</li>
<li><strong>Award review.</strong> Before anyone signs, we check that replacement cost, actual cash value and any depreciation holdback are spelled out so payment can follow cleanly.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Know the limits</b>The panel decides how much a covered loss costs. It does not decide coverage, and it does not award penalties or attorney’s fees. If the carrier has denied the claim, or you believe it handled the claim unfairly, talk with a Connecticut attorney first.</div>
<h2>When representation is a good fit</h2>
<p>Appraisal tends to work when the argument is about scope or price: how many slopes need shingles, whether the hardwood can be matched, what plaster repair costs in a 1920s colonial in West Hartford. It works poorly when the real argument is an exclusion, wear and tear, or a late-notice defense.</p>
<h2>How we keep numbers credible</h2>
<p>We tell you what we believe an umpire will accept, even when that is less than your contractor’s bid. Credibility with the umpire is the single biggest factor in how an award lands, and inflated estimates spend it fast.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Can my roofer be my appraiser?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Policies usually require a disinterested appraiser. Someone with a financial stake in the repair contract can give the carrier grounds to challenge the award, so most owners choose an independent appraiser.</p></div></details><details><summary>What if the insurer will not name its appraiser?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>The policy normally sets a time to name appraisers after a written demand. If one side does not act, the policy or a court may provide a path forward. We help you document every step, and an attorney can advise on enforcement.</p></div></details><details><summary>How long does appraisal take in Connecticut?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Straightforward residential claims often wrap up in six to ten weeks after both appraisers are named. Commercial losses and claims that go to an umpire hearing take longer.</p></div></details></div>
